WHY THE FAIL DOES NOT HOLD

(i) Criterion 4 defines "bind" operationally and exhaustively. It names
five probes that must fail and names the three mechanisms that make them
fail (body assertions scoped to tbody, header assertions to thead, colspan
counted against parsed <th>s). All five bind; all three mechanisms are
present and load-bearing. The written standard notes the swapped-cell probe
was NOT among the enumerated criteria. Attempt 2's brief was a remediation
brief written after attempt 1 precisely to make the bar decidable; reading
"bind" as "no surviving mutant exists" makes the bar unbounded, and under
that reading no attempt at any task could ever be accepted, because a
further surviving mutant is always constructible (swap the two glossary
<dd> bodies, swap the two <th> Tips, and so on).

(ii) DECISIVE: the gap is pre-existing and table-wide, not introduced by
T14. I swapped the two PRE-EXISTING cells — End Portfolio
({{formatMoney $c.EndingPortfolioReal}}) and Lifetime Tax
({{formatMoney $c.LifetimeTaxReal}}) — and ran
`go test -count=1 ./internal/handlers/whatif/ ./internal/templates/`:
both packages ok. So column-identity binding is absent for every column in
this table, and has been since before T14. checker-second is holding T14's
two new columns to a standard that none of the table's other five columns
meets and that the brief did not ask for. Failing attempt 2 on it would
require the worker to build a header-indexed cell-assertion harness that
was never specified, for two columns, while five neighbouring columns keep
the same gap — an inconsistent and unrequested remedy.

(iii) checker-second's own framing concedes the point. Its root-cause
paragraph faults "the task description's claim that the tests verify these
two new columns 'as two table columns after Lifetime Tax'" as an
overstatement. But that phrase in the task is a RENDERING requirement, and
the rendering requirement is met — verified above from actual output. The
overstatement, if any, is in a PASS narrative, not in the work. A checker
may not fail the worker for a claim the worker did not make.

CREDIT WHERE DUE. checker-second's finding is true, reproducible, honestly
scoped (it lists seven checks that passed before naming the one that
failed), and it restored and sha256-verified the template. This is the
adversarial lane working as designed. The finding should be RECORDED as
follow-up work — a small helper that parses the outer thead into an index
map and asserts each row's cell at the header's index, applied to the whole
tax-optimizer table, not just the two new columns. That is a new task, not
a defect in T14.2.

RULING: OVERRULE. The contested FAIL is set aside. Attempt 2 meets all five
written criteria; the column-identity gap is a genuine but out-of-scope,
pre-existing, non-user-visible coverage limitation and belongs in a
follow-up task.
